  "description": "Heritage short command aliases execute through the identical Core code path (issue #668, slice H3; execution proof for lt completed by the #672 conformance audit): the ten aliases `fd`/`bk`/`lt`/`rt`/`pu`/`pd`/`st`/`ht`/`cs`/`pr` are 'alternate spellings only — no new semantics' (spec/conformance.md#heritage), so this program's full event stream is byte-identical (payloads included) to the same program written with the Core names `forward`/`back`/`left`/`right`/`pen_up`/`pen_down`/`show_turtle`/`hide_turtle`/`clear_screen`/`print`. Both turn aliases are exercised: `rt` and `lt` run back-to-back inside the `spin` procedure body, and their `turn` events carry only the resulting headings (90 then 60) with no alias spelling in any payload — proving lt canonicalizes to `left` exactly as the other nine aliases canonicalize to their Core names. The remaining aliases are exercised in awkward positions the reader must still canonicalize: nested inside a `[ … ]` repeat body (`pu`/`fd`/`pd`/`bk`) and inside a procedure body (`rt`/`lt`/`fd` in `spin`), not just at top level. Every `move`/`turn`/`pen-change`/`visibility-change`/`clear`/`print` payload, and the `procedure-enter`/`procedure-exit` names, carry the canonical Core name because the runtime normalizes the alias to its `canonical` at a single dispatch chokepoint before any executor or event runs. The runtime is profile-blind (the reader records `canonical` unconditionally), so `data`/`heritage` need not be listed for execution — but they are, to document that these spellings are the Heritage profile's.",
